---
title: Mashed potatoes
description: Lesson 5.3. Cooking starch evenly, and why mash turns to glue.
servings: 4
time: 35 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, starch]
lesson: "5.3"
technique: boiling and mashing
status: draft
---

> Technique: cooking a starchy vegetable evenly, then handling it
> gently. Mash goes wrong when the outside of the potato overcooks
> before the middle is done.

Peel @potatoes{800%g}(Rooster, Maris Piper, Yukon Gold or russet; not red-skinned salad potatoes) and cut them into slices 1 cm thick.

Bring @water{1.5%litres} to a boil in a #large pot{} with @salt{1%tbsp}. Add the potato slices and simmer for ~{12%minutes}, until the point of a knife goes through a slice with no resistance.

> Why thin slices into boiling water: a thin slice cooks evenly from
> edge to edge. The old rule, large chunks started in cold water, was a
> way of limiting how much the outside overcooked while the middle
> caught up. Slices remove the problem and take less than half the
> time.
-- source: Lan Lam, "Mashed Potatoes That Break the Rules", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-12-05, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/6444-mashed-potatoes-that-break-the-rules

> Science: a potato's starch sits in granules inside its cells. As it
> cooks the granules swell with water and release amylose, which
> thickens whatever liquid it meets. When the outside of a potato
> overcooks, it turns to mush and sheds that sticky starch, and the
> loose starch is what makes mash gluey. Look up: starch
> gelatinization, amylose.
-- source: Bashir and Aggarwal, "Physicochemical, structural and functional properties of native and irradiated starch: a review", Journal of Food Science and Technology 56(2):513-523, 2019, https://doi.org/10.1007/s13197-018-3530-2 ; Lan Lam, "How to Make 15-Minute Mashed Potatoes", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-12-06, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/6640-15-minute-mashed-potatoes ("sloughed off starch that makes the mash gluey")

Drain the potatoes well and return them to the pot over low heat for ~{1%minute}, shaking the pot, to dry them.

Push the potatoes through a #potato ricer{} back into the pot, or mash them with a masher until just smooth.

> Why a ricer: in the test kitchen's comparison it gave the lightest,
> fluffiest mash, with a food mill next.
-- source: "Fluffy Mashed Potatoes", America's Test Kitchen, 2012-11-15, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/recipes/4135-fluffy-mashed-potatoes

Stir in @butter{60%g}(melted), then @milk{120%ml}(warm), then @salt{1/2%tsp}. Taste and adjust.

> Next time, without the recipe: slice the potatoes thin, cook them in
> boiling water until a knife slides through, dry them, press them
> rather than beat them, and add butter before milk.
